  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nClusters of nocturnal thunderstorm development is already\nunderway early this morning, setting the stage for what is\nexpected to be an active weather day. This will be under the\nguise of mid-level ripples of shortwave energy traveling along a\nwest-east oriented frontal boundary that is progged to slowly\nmigrate southward throughout the day. \n\nFor this morning's convective trends, closely monitoring rapid\ndevelopment of a thunderstorm complex evolving over northeast\nIN/northwest OH and pushing southward. Hi-res CAMs seem to have\na decent handle on this feature, which projects an arrival time\ninto the Ohio River Valley as early as 4 to 5 AM. At the time of\nwriting, the leading edge of this cluster has had history of\nproducing 50mph+ winds, which has warranted a Severe\nThunderstorm Watch upstream in western and central Ohio. \n\nThe progression of these storms over the next few hours will be\ntelling for what may occur here in our forecast area during the\npredawn hours into Saturday morning. Main concern with storms \nearly this morning will be the damaging wind threat along its \npath. If future projections hold, storms will reach the\nCharleston metro area shortly after sunrise and spread down into\nthe coalfields and mountains through the morning.\n\nAfter a small reprieve, renewed chances of showers and storms \narea anticipated to transpire heading into the afternoon amid \nstrong daytime heating and increasing instability. Some \nuncertainty exists for whether early morning convection \ncomplexes spoil destabilization during peak heating hours, \nespecially if lingering overcast rules the roost. However,\nforecast simulated reflectivity models hint at additional\ndevelopment over Indiana and Ohio once again spilling into West\nVirginia by this afternoon and evening. This will once again\npose concern for damaging winds, large hail, and localized flash\nflooding.\n\nAs high pressure grows over the Great Lakes, the cold front will\nbe forced further southward late tonight. This will quickly\ntaper down the severe risk and limit precipitation to the higher\nterrain by early Sunday morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe arrival of high pressure from the Great Lakes region,\naccompanied by dry air, will bring an end to the active weather\npattern beginning on Sunday. This feature slides into the New\nEngland and Mid-Atlantic regions on Labor Day and for the first\nhalf of the week, remaining in close vicinity to the Central \nAppalachians to yield a stretch of dry weather for Sunday\nthrough Tuesday. Seasonable temperatures, coupled with lower\nhumidity, promotes pleasant conditions throughout this time\nframe. Next chance for precipitation slides down along a cold\nfront late midweek.",
